Pathfinder Mini - Core Architecture
Design Philosophy

There are four kinds of data in the game:

Templates (never change)
Persistent Data (saved with the game)
World Data (exists while a map is loaded)
Systems (operate on the other three)

A piece of information should have exactly one owner.

Templates

Templates are immutable definitions.

They are compiled into the game and never change.

Monster
Goblin Archer
Goblin Spearman
Goblin Chief
Wolf
Skeleton

Contains:

Name
Sprite
Ability Scores
Speed
BAB
AC
Saves
Weapon
Armor
Challenge Rating
XP Reward
Loot Table
AI Type
Special Abilities

Monsters never gain XP.

Monsters never level.

Monsters never own inventory.

Item

Contains:

Name
Type
Weight
Value
Damage
Armor Bonus
Description

Never changes.

Ability

Contains:

Name
School
Range
Damage
Saving Throw
Description

Never changes.

Persistent Data

Persistent data is saved with the game.

Character

The Character is the permanent record of the player's adventure.

Contains:

Name
Class
Level
XP
Ability Scores
Skills
Equipment
Inventory
Known Spells
HP
MP
Conditions that persist outside combat

The Character is the single source of truth for the player.

Nothing else permanently stores this information.

World Data

World data exists only while a map is loaded.

When the player leaves the map, this data may disappear.

Entity

An Entity represents something that exists on a map.

Contains:

Position
Facing
Sprite
Active
Visibility
Turn State
Runtime combat data
Pointer to Character or
Pointer to Monster template

Entities do not own:

XP
Equipment
Inventory
Skills
Class
Monster Entity Runtime

A monster entity only stores information that changes.

Examples:

Current HP
Current Conditions
Initiative
Temporary Effects

Everything else comes from the Monster template.

When a monster dies:

Roll Loot Table.
Award XP.
Remove Entity.

The Monster template remains unchanged.

Combat

Combat is a rules engine.

Combat owns no permanent game data.

Combat performs:

Initiative
Turn Order
Movement
Attacks
Damage Rolls
Saving Throws
Conditions
End of Turn Effects

Combat never permanently stores:

HP
XP
Inventory
Equipment

Combat modifies the owning object.

Examples:

Player takes damage
        ↓
Character.currentHP changes
Goblin takes damage
        ↓
Monster Entity.currentHP changes

Combat asks helper functions for information.

Examples:

getAttackBonus(entity)

getArmorClass(entity)

getSpeed(entity)

applyDamage(entity)

isDead(entity)

Combat should never care whether the entity is a player or a monster.

Loot

Loot comes from Monster templates.

Each monster has a Loot Table.

Example:

Goblin Archer

70% Nothing

15% Healing Potion

10% Arrows

5% Random Goblin Weapon

Loot is generated when the monster dies.

Monsters do not carry inventories.

XP

XP comes from Monster templates.

Example:

Goblin Archer

XP Reward = 135

When the monster dies:

player.xp += monster.xpReward;

No Entity stores XP.

Saving

The save file stores:

Character
Story Progress
Completed Quests
World Flags

The save file does not store:

Combat
Temporary Monster Entities
Turn Order

Maps recreate entities when entered.

Ownership Rules

Every piece of data has exactly one owner.

Data	Owner
XP	Character
Level	Character
Equipment	Character
Inventory	Character
Skills	Character
HP (Player)	Character
HP (Monster)	Monster Entity
Position	Entity
Facing	Entity
Initiative	Entity
Conditions	Entity (combat/runtime) or Character (persistent), depending on duration
Loot Table	Monster Template
XP Reward	Monster Template
Sprite	Monster Template or Character/Class
AI	Monster Template
Guiding Principles
One owner per piece of data.
Templates are immutable.
The Character is the player's save data.
Entities are the physical things on the current map.
Combat is only a rules engine.
Monster templates define what a monster is; monster entities represent one instance of that monster in the world.
Avoid copying data unless there is a clear gameplay reason.

I think this should become our project's "constitution." Whenever we're unsure where a new piece of data belongs, we can come back to these rules and ask, Who owns this? If the answer isn't obvious, that's a sign the design needs another look before we write code.

There's only one point I'd like us to revisit when we start the refactor: conditions. I intentionally left room for them to exist in either the Entity (temporary combat effects like Shaken for one encounter) or the Character (persistent effects like Disease or Curse that survive leaving the map). Everything else in this document, though, I think gives us a solid foundation to build the rest of the game.
